Hi Dave,

Thank you for your insight. Yes, I also thought this myself, as the spatial extent wasn't as large as other examples I have seen in this group. Here is my code and the error that occurs when I try to download the full year -

# Map coords
xmn <- -5.3
xmx <- -2.6
ymn <- 51.4
ymx <- 53.4

# Temporal extent
st_time <- as.POSIXlt("2023-01-01 00:00", tz = "UTC")

en_time <- as.POSIXlt("2023-12-31 23:00", tz = "UTC")

# Set file name and path

file_prefix <- "era5_WJD23"
file_path <- getwd()

# Build request and execute

req <- build_era5_request(xmin = xmn, xmax = xmx,
                          ymin = ymn, ymax = ymx,
                          start_time = st_time,
                          end_time = en_time,
                          outfile_name = file_prefix)

request_era5(request = req, uid = uid, out_path = file_path)

Here is the resulting error after 1-2 minutes of running the request -

- timeout set to 5.0 hours Error in warn_or_error(error_msg, error = fail_is_error) : Data transfer failed with permanent client error: the request you have submitted is not valid. Reason given: Request too large. Requesting 105120 items, limit is 60000. More information at

I tried decreasing the spatial extent, but the number of items requested stayed the same (105120). I even tried a very small spatial extent with the following coordinates only to receive the same error as above -

xmn <- -4.0
xmx <- -3.9
ymn <- 52.1
ymx <- 52.2

Only by altering the temporal extent could I decrease the number of requested items to under the limit of 60000 and successfully download data.

Thank you for any help and further insight,

Shane

On Wednesday, May 29, 2024 at 3:14:52 AM UTC+1 dkli...@gmail.com wrote:
> Quick note, Shane: looking at your spatial extent, the ERA5 netCDF downloaded via request_era5 for one year shouldn't be much more than ~20 MB. If it's significantly bigger than that, do let me know (I'm the maintainer for the mcera5 package, which includes request_era5).
>
> Perhaps, you may have received a timeout error upon attempting to download a full year. If so, this doesn't have much to do with how large the download request is, as it does the global usage of the ECMWF Climate Data Store API by other people at the time of your request. As a result download times can be stochastic – sometimes a file may take hours, another day the same file only a few minutes.
>
> Best,
> Dave

